+# Single Galaxy — Resource markets, settlement consumption and waste recovery
+**Decision:** 2026-11-01. **Code gate:** ECON-03b. **Multiplayer:** solo-first, invite-only.
+
+## Economy: markets without money
+- Local markets represent resource access inside a settlement/resource-center service area; they do **not** imply magically unlimited resources. At early game-start most markets have few offers and very limited surplus.
+- System markets connect settlements within the same star system; regional markets describe settlements in multiple systems sharing an authoritative region; inter-regional markets describe larger exchanges. All levels use the same material quantities and conservation rules, so viewing higher aggregates does not duplicate stock.
+- Colonists consume food, water, oxygen, medical support and eventually diverse household goods and services; industries consume feedstock, utilities, spares, fuel and machinery. Physical consumption creates recurring demand independent of ship destruction, combat and manufacturing activity.
+- No wages, chits, guys, premium currency or cash-out. Resource exchange can involve transfers, supply agreements, barter, shared storage or a colony's standing aid policy. The game does not require numerical prices to plan actual logistical quantities.
+- Every surplus must subtract reserves and commitments. The current survey uses **3 needs cycles** (60 game ticks) of food, water and oxygen as an *indicative* reserve. It does not certify adequacy: the underlying stocks are in distinct compartments and may require water pipes or worker hauling.
+- Market discovery is read-only. A potential opportunity is not a reserved contract, a transport route, proof of permission or an executed sale. No resource moves between stations simply because they appear on a common screen.
+- Geographic identifiers must come from an authoritative server/map, not a player's message. Current colony host only runs its local system; future actual regional route maps, transport capacity, schedules, ownership, demand forecasting, NPC standing orders and conservation-backed settlement receipts require separate gates.
+
+## Waste: optionally recoverable, never mandatory micromanagement
+- **Wastewater**: the existing powered site reclaimer processes 10 kg into 9 kg recovered water and 1 kg sludge; the onboard recycler and farm process water under their own existing rules. This is game-balanced mass flow, not an assertion of potable wastewater without treatment equipment.
+- **Residential biological waste**: a constructed, powered site reclaimer additionally converts up to 10 kg of its source-separated organic-waste stream per compartment per needs cycle: 8 kg compost + 2 kg local residual sludge. The output remains in the original compartment. There is no invented pipe or free cross-site delivery. Reclamation does not run without the installed powered machine.
+- **Blackwater and medical waste**: visually abstractable in normal play, but physically distinct from clean source-separated compost. Medical waste remains isolated. Unprocessed residues do not simply become safe fertilizer.
+- **Steam**: once we have real boiler/condenser/thermal loops, condensers can return some water to their actual reservoirs. Steam is a working fluid, not a consumable fuel. Heat and power must be tracked separately from mass.
+- **Ordinary solid trash**: future sorted reclaimers can convert appropriate components into secondary feedstock plus unrecoverable residuals; there is no universal 100% trash-to-material machine.
+- **Player experience**: resource centers can abstract within-area connections; a utility overlay allows deliberate detail. NPCs can operate waste collection and treatment. Automatic waste disappearance is a visual convenience, not a resource-spawning process. When a future discard sink removes mass from play, it should be separately accounted rather than silently violating the physical ledger.
+
+## ECON-03b implementation boundary
+`galaxy::resource_market::survey` reads the authoritative simulation for built sites only. It reports stocks, NPC food/water/oxygen consumption, three-period reserve targets, shortages, potential surplus, waste streams, geographic aggregates and potential source-to-destination opportunities. The scan is deterministic and does not mutate world state. It does **not** yet execute shipments or contracts, and indicative totals can include tanks/storage that are not connected for transport.
+
+The colony host emits bounded `RESOURCE_MARKET` read-only telemetry per built outpost every 20 ticks, initially for food, water, oxygen and compost. Geographic data beyond the local host's single system is implemented as a generic survey classification, not an online galaxy service.
+
+## Acceptance after this gate
+1. Transactional, permissioned, conservation-safe NPC hauling and storage transfer between actual source and destination inventory compartments.
+2. Transport-capable market offers with physical reservations, expiry, receipt journal, no duplicated commitments across competing markets, and local-to-system scopes.
+3. Real regional geography and route availability, hazards and throughput; inter-regional contracts only when physical logistics exist.
+4. Waste collection jobs, compost pickup and farm fertilizer replenishment; recoverable scrap/trash handling with losses; future condenser steam recirculation.
+5. User-facing market board and shortage indicators, plus early-game experience that remains playable without an active public market.
